<DocsPage id="models">

          <p>
            Connect a provider account or API key to start. From 1.99.99, Vector also offers guarded free-model
            access through your own free OpenRouter account, with setup and limits described below.
          </p>

          <h2 id="byok">Bring your own key</h2>
          <p>
            Connect a provider in Settings, or run <code>vector providers login</code> in the terminal, then
            select a model in the session composer. Model availability, context limits, pricing, and
            rate limits come from the provider. Vector's usage view tracks the information returned by
            those sessions.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            From 1.99.99, Vector no longer loads the former third-party hosted providers, even with an API key.
            Choose another provider before starting a task. Sign in with ChatGPT, turned off in desktop 1.99.104 and 1.99.105, is available again from the next desktop release: connect OpenAI, choose ChatGPT Pro/Plus, and use the GPT models your ChatGPT plan includes, or connect OpenAI with an API key. Native
            Copilot, xAI, GitLab OAuth, Poe and DigitalOcean sign-in flows are paused pending Vector's own registrations;
            API-key methods remain where supported.
            External Claude Code, Codex,
            and Cursor runtimes still use your own installed CLIs and their authentication.
          </p>

          <h2 id="free-models">Free models inside of Vector</h2>
          <p>
            From 1.99.99, Vector offers guarded access through your own free OpenRouter account.
            In Settings → Providers, choose Connect OpenRouter, then choose an eligible model under
            Free models inside of Vector, marked Your OpenRouter account. This personal section works while
            Vector's shared allowance is off. Its initial selection is limited to explicit free variants with
            online, tool-capable, zero-price endpoints in OpenRouter's zero data retention (ZDR) list.
            It does not include every model OpenRouter lists as free.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep that account free: do not buy credits, add a payment method, enable automatic top-ups,
            attach paid provider keys through OpenRouter's BYOK settings, or enable default or enforced paid plugins.
            OpenRouter currently lists up to 50 requests a day and 20 a minute without a credit purchase;
            availability can be lower, and a coding task can consume many requests. At a limit, wait for the reset.
            The guarded route stops when no eligible zero-price endpoint is available and does not switch to
            a paid model or provider. <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/api_reference/limits">Check OpenRouter's current limits.</a>
          </p>
          <p>
            Prompts are processed by OpenRouter and the selected model provider. The guarded route enforces ZDR and
            denies data collection using OpenRouter's routing controls. This relies on
            <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/api-reference/endpoints/preview-the-impact-of-zdr-on-the-available-endpoints">OpenRouter's endpoint classification</a>,
            not an independent policy audit. It does not promise zero retention for Vector's local history,
            OpenRouter account logging or other connected services. Review those settings before sending confidential code.
            Other provider connections have their own terms. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop 1.99.8, do not
            include this route. The shared service remains off.
          </p>
          <p>
            For guarded free models, save an API key through Settings → Providers or set
            <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code> in the engine environment. A key stored only in a project's
            <code>provider.openrouter.options.apiKey</code> does not enable this route; migrate it to a provider
            connection or environment variable without committing it to the project.
            <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/provider-selection">OpenRouter routing documentation</a>
            describes the ZDR and data-collection controls.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-models">Models in the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector models</code> lists the supported model catalog. Connect a provider with <code>vector providers login</code> to use its models. Pass a provider id to filter and <code>--verbose</code> for metadata such as per-token cost. The catalog is bundled with the release. From 1.99.99, <code>--refresh</code> only fetches when a mirror is explicitly configured with <code>VECTOR_MODELS_URL</code>, <code>VECTOR_MODELS_PATH</code> selects a local catalog, and <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_MODELS_FETCH=1</code> disables refresh.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector models
vector models anthropic --verbose</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            From 1.99.99, your personal account adds eligible models under
            <strong>Free models inside of Vector</strong>, marked Your OpenRouter account, independently of the
            disabled shared allowance. Display names omit <code>:free</code>; selectors retain it. Human guidance
            goes to stderr, while stdout keeps plain model selectors. Connect your own account with
            <code>vector providers login --provider openrouter --method "Connect OpenRouter"</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Browser authorization returns to the engine's localhost. If you use a remote engine without access
            to that callback port, connect through the API-key method or set <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code> on
            that engine. A project-only provider key does not enable guarded free models. Releases before 1.99.99,
            such as CLI 1.99.7 and desktop 1.99.8, do not include this setup.
          </p>
          <p>
            Models that expose reasoning effort take <code>--variant</code>. The level resolves from the flag, then your saved preference, then what the session last used, and your choice is kept per provider and model.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
